<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Niger River</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Niger</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Niger River is the principal river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, flowing in a great arc from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> highlands through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahel">Sahel</a> to the Atlantic in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>. It was a vital artery for agriculture, trade and military movement. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songhai-empire">Songhai</a> empires grew along its middle course, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto</a> states lay in its lower basin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/niger-river.jpg" alt="Niger River" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NigerTZai · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Niger,_Niamey,_Pont_Kennedy_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Control of this route, along which gold from the Bambuk and Bure fields of the upper Senegal and <b>Niger</b> flowed northward to Mediterranean markets, gave the movement a financial resource that would underpin its imperial ambitions for decades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almoravid-empire">Almoravid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Bamana Empire, or Kingdom of Ségou, grew up among Bamana farmers on the middle <b>Niger</b> in the political vacuum left by the fall of the Songhay Empire to Morocco in 1591.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bamana-empire">Bamana Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1880s and 1890s French columns pushed east to the <b>Niger</b>, defeating the Tukulor state of Ahmadu, taking Timbuktu in 1894 and fighting a long war against Samori Ture, who was captured in 1898, while other expeditions conquered the Kingdom of Dahomey in 1892–1894 and occupied the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hausa kingdoms grew out of farming communities on the savanna of what is now northern Nigeria and southern <b>Niger</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hausa-kingdoms">Hausa kingdoms · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…then went north-west into the savanna to found Oyo, which grew into an empire stretching at its height from the right bank of the <b>Niger</b> to the left bank of the Volta, one of the most powerful of Africa's medieval states until its collapse in the mid-nineteenth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-ife">Kingdom of Ife · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Kano became the leading commercial city of the central Sudan, linking the kola country of Gonja and the <b>Niger</b> valley with Bornu and the trans-Saharan caravan routes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-kano">Kingdom of Kano · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
